exercises.solution:
```python
def build_hours(volume_cm3, height_mm, layer_mm, rate_cm3_h, lasers, recoat_s):
    return volume_cm3 / (rate_cm3_h * lasers) + height_mm / layer_mm * recoat_s / 3600

def min_lasers(volume_cm3, height_mm, layer_mm, rate_cm3_h, recoat_s, target_h, max_lasers=64):
    for n in range(1, max_lasers + 1):
        if build_hours(volume_cm3, height_mm, layer_mm, rate_cm3_h, n, recoat_s) <= target_h:
            return n
    return None
```

For the lesson's chamber (293 cm³ to scan, 6250 layers at 9 s), a 20-hour target needs 5 lasers; a 15-hour target is impossible because recoating alone takes 15.6 hours, however many lasers you add. Ten chambers on one plate in 40 hours need 8. Since build time falls monotonically with the number of lasers, you could also solve for $n$ directly, $n = \lceil V/(r(T - T_\text{recoat})) \rceil$ when $T > T_\text{recoat}$; the loop is clearer and handles the impossible case naturally.

