def precision_recall(flags, truth):
    """Return (precision, recall); precision is None when nothing is flagged."""
    raise NotImplementedError

exercises.reference:
def precision_recall(flags, truth):
    tp = sum(1 for f, t in zip(flags, truth) if f and t)
    fp = sum(1 for f, t in zip(flags, truth) if f and not t)
    fn = sum(1 for f, t in zip(flags, truth) if t and not f)
    precision = tp / (tp + fp) if tp + fp else None
    return precision, tp / (tp + fn)

exercises.solution:
```python
def precision_recall(flags, truth):
    tp = sum(1 for f, t in zip(flags, truth) if f and t)
    fp = sum(1 for f, t in zip(flags, truth) if f and not t)
    fn = sum(1 for f, t in zip(flags, truth) if t and not f)
    precision = tp / (tp + fp) if tp + fp else None
    return precision, tp / (tp + fn)
```

The two extreme detectors in the tests show why both numbers are needed: flagging nothing has undefined precision and zero recall (yet high accuracy when defects are rare), while flagging everything has perfect recall and precision equal to the defect rate. A useful detector is somewhere between, and where exactly depends on the cost of a missed defect relative to a needless inspection.
